msgid "``_templates/discussion.typ`` and ``_templates/theme.typ``, the editable PDF layout and shared theme copied from the binary;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:34
msgid "``registry.rst`` and ``bundle.rst``, the generated index;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:35
msgid "``records/XXXXX-eds-process.rst``, a draft of our review process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:37
msgid "We edit the process draft to say how our project decides. To start from our own documents, we pass ``--config=FILE`` (a ``discussion.toml``), ``--process=FILE`` (the process text, from its title on), and ``--template=FILE`` (a record template with ``:eds-*:`` fields). ``--labels=a,b`` limits the labels records may carry. ``init`` checks the new catalog before it moves it into place. It never writes into a folder that has files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:44
msgid "Write and review"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:47
msgid "We run the commands in the catalog folder, or in any folder inside it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:58
msgid "A new draft has the number ``XXXXX``. Promotion gives it the next number and opens it for discussion. A state change needs ``--reason``, and ``committed`` also needs ``--evidence``. ``--dry-run`` shows a change without making it. These are the defaults. The next section changes them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:63
msgid "A record starts with its metadata, one field per line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:77
msgid "Authors and labels are JSON lists. ``last-updated`` may stand for ``updated``. A record may also carry ``category``, ``status``, and ``summary``. ``guidedog dsc`` keeps ``status`` in step with the state. HTML and PDF show the fields as Number, Title, Authors, Updated, Labels, and so on, with the lists as plain text."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:82
msgid "Configure the lifecycle and numbering"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:85
msgid "``discussion.toml`` holds the catalog's settings. ``init`` writes every one of them, with comments. ``guidedog dsc config`` prints the settings in effect. A catalog without the file follows the defaults below. They are the process of Guidedog's own GDS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:122
msgid "``[workflow.states.NAME]`` defines a state. ``next`` lists the states the ``state`` command may move a record to. ``evidence = true`` asks for ``--evidence`` on a move into the state. ``status`` is the text a record's optional ``status`` field takes there. A state with no ``next`` is closed. Promotion is the only way from the draft state to the open state, because promotion gives the number. Without a number, a draft may move only to the states its own ``next`` lists, such as ``abandoned``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:129
msgid "Numbers have at least ``width`` digits, padded with zeros. A number that needs more digits gets them, so 10000 follows 9999, and lists sort by value. Promotion numbers from ``start``. ``limit``, when set, is the highest number it gives. Changing ``start`` or ``limit`` later is safe. Changing ``width`` is not, once records are numbered: their numbers would no longer match it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:135
msgid "``guidedog dsc`` checks the file whenever it reads the catalog. A mistake names the line and what to write instead:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:150
msgid "The process draft that ``init`` writes describes the lifecycle we configured. It has a table of the states and a diagram of the moves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:153
msgid "Build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:161
msgid "``guidedog dsc build`` writes the website to ``_build/html`` and one PDF per record to ``_build/pdf``. Naming a record writes only its PDF, to ``_build/selected``, and leaves the complete output alone. Warnings stop the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:165
msgid "The build is guidedog's own: the same build as ``guidedog build``, with the catalog as the project. Graphviz, Typst, and the other tools it needs are guidedog's too. An older catalog of Typst records compiles each record with Typst; ``--typst=PATH`` names a Typst program to use instead of the embedded compiler."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:167
msgid "Discussion PDFs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:170
msgid "Each RST record uses an RFC-style paper layout. The record opens with a metadata panel, state badges, a short status note and compact contents, followed by its text. Numbered sections flow continuously. Running headers identify the catalog and record, and footers show the page number and total. Tables and captioned listings can continue across pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:172
msgid "The PDF builder recognizes the leading metadata, such as ``:eds-number:`` and ``:eds-title:``, for any catalog prefix. Existing catalogs without a copied discussion template use the current embedded layout. We keep existing template customizations until we replace or remove their files. Ordinary books keep their book template."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:174
msgid "The layout is embedded in the Guidedog binary. ``guidedog dsc init`` copies ``_templates/discussion.typ`` and its shared ``_templates/theme.typ`` for customization. Existing catalogs without a copy use the embedded layout. It defines a ``book`` function with the usual PDF parameters and an additional ``record`` dictionary. ``record.id`` is the readable identifier, ``record.catalog`` is the project name, and ``record.fields`` contains pairs of readable field labels and values. All values arrive as quoted strings. The source heading supplies the title and its anchor. The template reads the catalog metadata without assuming a fixed prefix or lifecycle. Its optional ``contents`` and ``memo`` parameters control the contents and status note. :doc:`../templates` describes the shared PDF parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:176
msgid "An ``--untrusted`` build uses the built-in discussion layout instead of running a project template. A record included as a chapter of a larger book keeps its metadata in that chapter; the root document selects the book's layout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:178
msgid "When an update is interrupted"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:181
msgid "Commands that change records hold the catalog's lock, ``.eds.lock``. An update of several files first writes a journal, ``.eds-journal.json``. If it stops part way, the next command says so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:190
msgid "Recovery puts back the files as they were. It refuses to overwrite a file someone edited since. ``guidedog dsc`` also checks each change before it writes anything. It refuses an empty or cut-short record, and an index without its generated region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:194
msgid "``guidedog dsc`` keeps only each record's metadata in memory. It reads a record whole only to change it. So checking a large catalog stays fast and small."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:197
msgid "Use the library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:200
msgid "The commands are one Rust crate, ``gd-discussion``. Its ``README.md`` shows how to load a catalog, plan a change, and apply it from our own program:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:218
msgid "``ds::plugin()`` is the whole ``guidedog dsc`` command line, as a plugin. A ``guidedog`` of our own links it beside its other plugins (see :doc:`../plugins`). The design notes are in :doc:`../internals/discussion`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:1
msgid "Document a codebase"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:4
msgid "A public name needs a contract. We explain its input, its result, its ownership, and the conditions under which it fails. A signature alone is not a manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:7
msgid "Static extraction architecture"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:10
msgid "Guidedog extracts API documentation by parsing source files into abstract syntax trees. It never runs or imports our code. So there are no side effects, no runtime to install, and no foreign dependencies to resolve:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:16
msgid "Code source trees are parsed into ASTs statically, transformed into virtual API documents, and integrated into cross-references and objects.inv."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:16
msgid "Guidedog static code documentation architecture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:65
msgid "Rust"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:74
msgid "Guidedog reads each crate's source with tree-sitter and follows its module tree the way rustc does: ``Cargo.toml``'s ``[lib] path``, then ``src/lib.rs`` or ``src/main.rs``. It does not compile, run, or expand the documented crate. Items made by macros or build scripts are therefore absent. A ``rust_autoapi_crates`` entry may also be a single root ``.rs`` file or a folder of loose ``.rs`` files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:80
msgid "Rustdoc's rules decide what appears. A ``pub`` item reachable through ``pub`` modules is documented in its module. ``pub use`` of an item from a private module documents it under the re-exported name. ``#[doc(hidden)]`` items, ``#[cfg(test)]`` modules and tests are left out. ``rust_autoapi_options`` adds ``private-members`` (everything, with its visibility shown), ``hidden-members``, or ``noindex``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:86
msgid "Doc comments are CommonMark. Guidedog translates them into the page. ``# Examples`` and the other headings become rubrics. Fenced code follows rustdoc's language rules. Intra-doc links (``[`Parser`]``, ``[Parser::next]``) become ``rust`` domain references. A link into the crate that names nothing is a ``rust.autodoc.link`` warning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:91
msgid "The pages are written under ``rust_autoapi_root``. There is an index page, then one page per documented module (``my_crate.rst``, ``my_crate/parser.rst``). With ``rust_autoapi_add_toctree_entry`` (the default), the index joins the root document's toctree. With ``rust_autoapi_generate_api_docs = false``, Guidedog reads the settings without generating pages. :doc:`../topics/domains` describes the ``rust`` directives and roles. Hand-written pages can use them too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:98
msgid "Odin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:107
msgid "Guidedog reads Odin source by static analysis, across every build target at once. It does not compile or run the documented package. The generated pages join the document graph and object inventory. See :doc:`../odin` for directives, signatures, and options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:112
msgid "Python"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:120
msgid "Guidedog parses Python source rather than importing it. This avoids import side effects. It also means runtime-generated objects may be absent. When such objects are part of the public interface, we describe them explicitly. See :doc:`../autodoc` for the exact discovery rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:125
msgid "Link across projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:128
msgid "We publish the generated ``objects.inv`` with the site. An intersphinx mapping in another project can then resolve our names. Explicit mappings keep builds reproducible. See :doc:`../intersphinx`."
msgstr ""

