msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:7
msgid "Use the official ``insanai/homebrew-guidedog`` tap on Apple silicon or Intel Macs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:15
msgid "Homebrew selects the release archive for our processor and checks its SHA-256. The executable has the Typst compiler and Graphviz built in, so it needs nothing else at run time. We do not need a Rust toolchain, a Python environment, or a separate Typst installation to build HTML and PDF. The release requires macOS 12 or later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:19
msgid "The package installs one executable, ``guidedog``. Its built-in plugins add GitHub Pages publishing (``guidedog publish pages`` and ``guidedog pages``; see :doc:`github-pages`), discussion catalogs (``guidedog dsc``; see :doc:`discussions`), and static site publishing (``guidedog ssg``; see :doc:`static-publishing`). The ``gd-graphdog`` library encapsulates Graphviz inside Guidedog."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:21
msgid "We create our first project in :doc:`../tutorial/first-project`. To update an installed release:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:29
msgid "Run ``brew test insanai/guidedog/guidedog`` to check the installed package by creating a project and building HTML and PDF."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:32
msgid "Build from source"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:35
msgid "Guidedog is a Cargo workspace. We need:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:37
msgid "a Rust toolchain at the version ``rust-version`` names in the root ``Cargo.toml`` (Rust 1.99 or newer; edition 2024), installed with ``rustup``;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:39
msgid "the Graphviz library, because Guidedog links it (``libgvc`` and ``libcgraph``) to draw graphs in process. Either install the system's development files (``libgraphviz-dev`` on Debian and Ubuntu, ``graphviz-devel`` on Fedora, ``graphviz`` from Homebrew on macOS), or build static libraries with ``cargo xtask graphviz`` (see below);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:40
msgid "a C compiler (``cc``, Xcode's command-line tools, or MSVC), which Cargo uses to build the tree-sitter grammars and the cryptography in the ``ring`` crate (TLS for HTTPS)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:42
msgid "Nothing else is built by hand. The Typst compiler (0.15.1), the tree-sitter grammars for Python and Rust, and the HTTP client (``ureq`` with ``rustls``) are ordinary Cargo dependencies, compiled with the workspace. HTTPS needs no ``curl`` program, OpenSSL or system certificate store: the root certificates are built in. There are no native build scripts to run first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:46
msgid "Release build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:49
msgid "From the repository root:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:57
msgid "This links the system's shared Graphviz libraries, so the program needs them at run time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:59
msgid "Static Graphviz"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:62
msgid "The release archives link Graphviz statically, so they need no Graphviz on the machine that runs them. We build the same way:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:70
msgid "``cargo xtask graphviz`` downloads Graphviz 16.1.0 and Expat 2.7.1, checks each against its pinned SHA-256, and builds static libraries into ``target/graphviz/16.1.0``, the folder it prints. It needs a C and C++ compiler, ``make``, ``curl``, ``tar`` and ``xz``, and takes a few minutes. The build has only what Guidedog uses: the dot and neato layout plugins (dot, neato, fdp, sfdp, circo, twopi, osage, patchwork) and the core renderers (SVG and DOT), with no X11, cairo, gd, ltdl or zlib. So a static build renders SVG and laid-out DOT, but not PNG."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:72
msgid "``GUIDEDOG_GRAPHVIZ_STATIC`` tells ``gd-graphdog``'s build script where the libraries are; the ``static-graphviz`` feature of ``gd-graphdog`` uses the default folder. ``guidedog --version`` then reports ``graphviz 16.1.0 (static)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:74
msgid "The executable is ``target/release/guidedog`` (``guidedog.exe`` on Windows). To run ``guidedog`` without its path, we put it on our ``PATH`` or install it into Cargo's binary folder:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:82
msgid "``guidedog formats`` lists the readers and renderers built in, and the Typst backend it found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:85
msgid "Typst: embedded or installed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:88
msgid "By default the Typst compiler is linked into ``guidedog`` (the ``embedded`` feature of the ``gd-typst`` crate). PDF then needs no other program. A build of ``gd-typst`` without that feature runs an installed ``typst`` program (0.15.1) instead. Either way, ``--typst=/absolute/path/to/typst`` (or ``typst`` in ``conf.toml``) selects a program explicitly, and ``guidedog --version`` names the backend in use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:94
msgid "The embedded compiler removes the need to start a separate process. It does not remove the compiler's own memory costs. ``--budget`` bounds what Guidedog itself holds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:97
msgid "Development checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:100
msgid "Before we propose a change, we run the same checks as the project's continuous integration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:110
msgid "``cargo run -p check`` is the source-limit checker in ``tools/check``: function and file lengths and line widths. ``cargo xtask tables --check`` checks that the generated data tables match the upstream data in ``tools/data``. ``cargo xtask --help`` lists the other repository tasks: the website, the fonts, and the release steps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:113
msgid "Built-in plugins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:116
msgid "The release has one executable. The discussion plugin adds ``guidedog dsc``. The static site plugin adds ``guidedog ssg``. The Pages plugin adds ``guidedog publish pages`` and ``guidedog pages`` maintenance commands. All three are linked into ``guidedog`` and use its services; no additional program or registration is needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:118
msgid "It keeps tokens in the system's credential store: the Keychain on macOS, the Credential Manager on Windows, and on Linux the Secret Service through the ``secret-tool`` program (``libsecret-tools``). It needs that program only when it first stores or reads a token."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:122
msgid "Embed the engine"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:125
msgid "The conversion engine is a library. A Rust program can convert text without the ``guidedog`` command, through the ``guidedoc`` crate and the reader and renderer crates it needs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/install.rst:152
msgid "``guidedoc_rst::reader()``, ``guidedoc_commonmark::MYST_READER``, ``guidedoc_typst::renderer()`` and ``guidedoc_text::renderer()`` join the registry the same way. For code highlighting, the ``guidedoc-defaults`` crate joins the highlighter to the renderers. We pass a ``guidedoc_defaults::HtmlHighlighter`` (or ``TypstHighlighter``) as ``highlighter`` in the request's ``RenderOptions``. The staged calls (``read_document``, ``transform_document``, ``check_render``, ``render_document``), the request fields, and the limits are in the crate's ``README.md``."
msgstr ""

