#: ../../howto/publish.rst:1
msgid "Build and publish documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:4
msgid "To publish documentation, we turn reStructuredText and Markdown sources into static files, check them, and deploy them to a host."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:7
msgid "Guidedog keeps building a site separate from deploying it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:9
msgid "**Building:** Guidedog reads our sources, compiles templates, and resolves cross-references. It writes a complete, checked folder of static HTML files or a typeset PDF book."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:11
msgid "**Publishing:** Our web server serves the static files, or we deploy them to a host such as GitHub Pages, an S3 bucket, or an internal file server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:14
msgid "The output is plain static files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and SVG). Any static web server (such as Nginx, Apache, or Caddy) can serve it. Production needs no Guidedog, no Python interpreter, and no running server process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:18
msgid "Atomic publication architecture"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:21
msgid "Guidedog publishes only complete output generations. The host finishes preparation before it decides a journaled commit. A failed preparation leaves the previous site intact."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:25
msgid "Guidedog prepares a complete generation before deciding a journaled commit:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:29
msgid "A complete staged generation is journaled and switched into place. Recovery finishes or restores an interrupted commit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:29
msgid "The atomic staging and generation switch process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:47
msgid "How atomic commits protect our site:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:49
msgid "**Staging:** All newly rendered pages are written to a hidden staging folder (``.<target>.next``). For the default builders it sits in the build folder. For an output we name with ``-o``, it sits inside the output's one cache folder beside it (``.<target>.guidedog``), so the output gains a single hidden neighbour."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:53
msgid "**Isolation:** The live output folder stays untouched while pages compile."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:54
msgid "**Failure safety:** If any page fails to render, or a warning becomes an error under ``-W``, the build abandons the staging folder. The live site stays unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:56
msgid "**Atomic swap:** Once all files are flushed, Guidedog switches the staging folder into place. Where the filesystem supports it, this is a single directory exchange."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:58
msgid "**Interrupted recovery:** If a crash interrupts the swap, the next build reads the durable journal. It settles the interrupted commit instead of guessing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:61
msgid "Standard build workflow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:64
msgid "To build both HTML and PDF outputs into the default project output directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:71
msgid "Output locations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:73
msgid "The HTML website is written to ``docs/manual/_build/html/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:74
msgid "The PDF book is written to ``docs/manual/_build/pdf/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:76
msgid "The ``-j auto`` flag parallelizes document parsing across all available CPU cores."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:78
msgid "Custom output directories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:81
msgid "To write the site to a folder of our choice (for example, in a CI/CD pipeline or a deployment script), we use the Sphinx-compatible command form:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:88
msgid "Guidedog enforces safety constraints on output paths:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:90
msgid "Choose a dedicated output folder, such as ``_build/html`` inside the project or ``build/site`` beside it. The output must not be the source root or one of its ancestors, because switching it would replace the source project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:93
msgid "For a custom destination such as ``build/site``, Guidedog keeps its internal caches in a sibling folder (such as ``build/.site.guidedog/.doctrees``). The published folder then holds only the static files we deploy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:97
msgid "Semantic checks and pre-flight validation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:100
msgid "To check a project without writing HTML files to disk, we use the ``dummy`` builder:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:107
msgid "The dummy builder:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:109
msgid "Reads and parses all documents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:110
msgid "Resolves all toctrees, headings, cross-references, and domain objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:111
msgid "Checks document references and domain objects (``-n`` enables nitpicky cross-reference checking). It does not make HTTP requests to test external URLs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:113
msgid "Exits with status 1 if any broken link or warning is found (``-W``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:115
msgid "In continuous integration, the dummy builder is a fast check to run before the full site build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:118
msgid "Reproducible build timestamps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:121
msgid "Documentation builds often embed timestamps (for example, in \"Last updated\" footer notices). When an automated deployment must give the same bytes on every run, we pin the clock with the standard ``SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`` environment variable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:129
msgid "When this variable is set, Guidedog uses the specified Unix epoch timestamp instead of the current system clock for all date formatting, publication eligibility checks, and template variables. We can also pass ``--now=2026-05-01T09:00:00Z`` to ``guidedog build``, ``guidedog serve``, or ``guidedog ssg build`` to evaluate scheduled ``:publish-at:`` and ``:expires-at:`` windows against an explicit RFC 3339 instant, or ``--preview`` to include drafts and future-scheduled pages in a local build. See :doc:`static-publishing`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:131
msgid "Deployment options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:134
msgid "Once the build succeeds, we deploy the static files to our host:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:136
msgid "**Static web servers (Nginx / Caddy / Apache):** Point the server's root directive directly to the built HTML directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:138
msgid "**Cloud storage buckets (AWS S3 / Cloudflare R2 / Google Cloud Storage):** Sync the contents of ``_build/html`` to our bucket with the cloud provider's CLI tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:140
msgid "**GitHub Pages:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:142
msgid "To deploy directly from our own machine, we run ``guidedog publish pages``. It asks for what is still missing (the repository, a token, the Pages settings), then builds and publishes in one push. ``guidedog pages`` offers the same steps one by one. See :doc:`github-pages`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:146
msgid "For automated deployments with GitHub Actions, ``guidedog pages workflow`` writes a workflow that builds and deploys the site. See :doc:`github-pages`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:148
msgid "For any other destination, we write a publisher plugin as described in :doc:`../plugins` and run it with ``guidedog publish --publisher=ID``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/publish.rst:151
msgid "On filesystems with directory exchange, the output path switches in one operation. The fallback uses two renames and can briefly leave that path absent. It still never exposes a partly assembled tree. Journal recovery finishes or restores an interrupted switch. A client making several requests can span two generations. The build does not promise one snapshot for a whole browsing session."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:1
msgid "Static websites and articles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:4
msgid "Dated articles, release notes, whitepapers and standalone landing pages share the normal HTML and PDF pipeline. Leading metadata fields declare when a page is eligible for publication, which layout it uses, and whether it also compiles to a standalone article PDF."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:6
msgid "Authoring and publication state"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:9
msgid "The ``guidedog ssg`` commands create and transition articles in place:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:16
msgid "Pass a ``.md`` path to create Markdown with YAML front matter instead of reStructuredText. For Markdown builds, include ``.md`` in ``source_suffix`` and enable ``myst_parser`` in ``extensions`` as usual. ``new`` creates a draft and refuses to overwrite an existing file. ``publish`` clears draft, schedule and expiration, retaining an existing past date or stamping the current UTC date when the source had none or a future date. ``draft`` withholds a source from subsequent release builds without discarding its existing date metadata. Version control stays in the author's hands; the commands never commit, tag or move files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:18
msgid "An article declares its metadata in the leading field list (or YAML front matter block):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:33
msgid "Date-only publication starts at midnight UTC. ``publish-at`` overrides that boundary with an RFC 3339 instant, and ``expires-at`` ends publication at an exclusive instant. Drafts, future articles and expired articles are excluded before navigation, search, feeds and source downloads are built. Publication metadata is source configuration and is excluded from gettext catalogs. Recognized publication fields supply layout data and are omitted from body rendering and search text, while unknown fields and ordinary later field lists remain content. To vary metadata for a separate language edition, edit that edition's source files. Invalid publication metadata stops the output commit and preserves the previous successful website."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:37
msgid "Sources with leading metadata pass through the build-clock gate; eligible documents produce HTML pages, optional article PDFs, collection archives and discovery feeds, while withheld documents stay in the private cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:37
msgid "The build-clock gate filters sources before navigation, collections, feeds and article PDFs are built."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:63
msgid "Use ``guidedog build html --preview`` (or ``guidedog serve --preview``) to inspect unpublished pages locally. When keeping both public and preview outputs on disk, write the preview to a separate output directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:67
msgid "Collections, archives and feeds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:70
msgid "Embed a chronological list of eligible articles in any page with ``.. postlist::``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:80
msgid "``:path:`` matches docnames without a source suffix. Category, author and series filters match exactly, and every listed tag must match. Articles are ordered by publication instant (newest first by default), with docname breaking ties; ``:sort: date`` places the oldest first. ``:style: cards`` adds a card-layout CSS class, and ``:excerpts:`` includes each article's introduction or plain-text summary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:82
msgid "Rich article introductions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:85
msgid "We place one ``.. more::`` boundary after the introduction to select a rich excerpt:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:108
msgid "The introduction retains paragraphs, inline markup, links, lists, images and other ordinary document nodes. Both ``postlist`` with ``:excerpts:`` and generated collection or taxonomy pages render it during the build. The full article retains everything on both sides of the boundary in HTML and PDF. The boundary emits no visible content. Drafts, scheduled articles and expired articles follow the same publication gate as other listings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:114
msgid "We put the boundary at document level, before any subsection, outside lists and other directives. It accepts no argument, option or body. A second boundary or a nested boundary produces a diagnostic. Literal ``.. more::`` text inside a code example remains literal text. An empty introduction is allowed. Toctrees, postlists and todolists inside an introduction are omitted from its excerpt to prevent recursive listings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:121
msgid "Without a boundary, the excerpt is the escaped plain-text ``:summary:`` in a paragraph, or empty when no summary is given. We keep ``:summary:`` independent of the rich introduction: feeds and description metadata continue to use that plain text. For a Tinkerer migration, we can retain ``.. more::`` and replace site-specific browser code with native ``postlist`` directives or collection templates. No runtime plugin or browser fetch is required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:126
msgid "A collection template can render the selected introduction directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:137
msgid "``post.excerpt_html`` is rendered HTML marked safe for the template engine. It follows the build's content policy, including restrictions on raw markup and unsafe URLs. Links, images and downloads are relative to the destination listing, including nested archives and pagination pages. Each listing occurrence has its own anchor namespace. Local references to included targets stay within the excerpt; references to targets or footnotes after the boundary link to the full article. We render a post's excerpt once per template occurrence; repeating the same ``post.excerpt_html`` value in a template also repeats its HTML IDs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:144
msgid "Enable site-wide discovery files in ``conf.toml``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:153
msgid "The HTML and dirhtml builders emit ``sitemap.xml``, ``rss.xml`` and ``atom.xml`` using the configured ``base_url`` (including any hosting prefix). Discovery files derive their timestamps from source metadata rather than the wall clock, producing identical bytes when eligible sources and metadata are unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:155
msgid "Generate taxonomy archives and paginated collections from the same ``[site]`` table:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:172
msgid "The collection starts at ``journal/index.html`` and continues at ``journal/page/2/index.html`` with relative previous and next links. A zero ``page_size`` keeps the collection on a single page. Each collection accepts ``path``, ``category``, ``tags``, ``author``, ``series`` and ``year`` filters. When ``feeds = true``, ``journal/rss.xml`` and ``journal/atom.xml`` contain the complete filtered selection across all pages. The collection layout defaults to ``collection.html`` unless ``template`` names an override. Templates receive the current page number, page count and selected posts in ``collection``, with ``collection.previous_url`` and ``collection.next_url`` set to relative URLs or ``None``. Each post entry provides ``title``, ``docname``, ``date``, ``url``, ``summary``, ``excerpt_html`` and typed ``meta`` values, including its optional ``pdf_url``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:174
msgid "Taxonomy archives are written at paths such as ``tags/rust/index.html`` and ``year/2026/index.html``. Headings preserve the original label text while filesystem paths escape uppercase, punctuation and non-ASCII bytes so distinct labels never collide on case-insensitive filesystems. Generated pages never overwrite an author-written page or another generated output. When publication state or collection membership shrinks, the output commit removes obsolete archive and pagination pages automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:176
msgid "Layouts and standalone article PDFs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:179
msgid "Dated articles select ``article.html`` by default; other pages select ``page.html``, which extends ``layout.html``. Any page can choose a custom template in ``templates_path`` with ``:template: landing.html``, and ``:nosidebar: true`` hides the built-in navigation sidebar. Templates receive dates, authors, tags, category, series, summary, draft state and PDF links in ``page.meta``. Article templates also receive ``page.older`` and ``page.newer`` in publication order, ``page.taxonomies`` mapping each enabled taxonomy to ``{name, url}`` entries, and ``page.word_count`` and ``page.reading_minutes`` (computed using ``myst_words_per_minute``, counting CJK characters individually)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:181
msgid "Setting ``:pdf: article`` compiles a standalone PDF next to the article's HTML page (``notes.pdf`` for ``html``, ``notes/index.pdf`` for ``dirhtml``). The built-in ``article.typ`` layout renders continuous prose with a title and author block, abstract, and running headers after page one. Override it by placing ``_templates/article.typ`` in the project with the same ``book`` function signature. The generated Typst source is saved under ``sources/`` in the output folder for inspection. Standalone article PDFs are produced by the ``html`` and ``dirhtml`` builders; ``singlehtml`` retains its single-page HTML behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:183
msgid "Starter sites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:186
msgid "Initialize a complete starter project with one of three presets:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:193
msgid "``project`` starts with a custom landing page and a documentation guide."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:194
msgid "``journal`` starts with a dated article, chronological cards, feeds and taxonomy archives."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:195
msgid "``research`` starts with a paper containing an abstract and mathematics, and builds its standalone PDF."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:197
msgid "All three presets copy editable HTML, CSS and Typst theme files and refuse to overwrite an existing destination. Update the example ``base_url`` in ``conf.toml`` before publishing feeds or sitemaps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:199
msgid "When no mounts are configured, ``guidedog ssg build`` builds the single project into ``_build/site`` by default. ``--out DIR`` selects another destination, ``--now RFC3339`` fixes the publication clock, and ``--preview`` includes withheld documents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:201
msgid "Mounted projects and editions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:204
msgid "Assemble multiple Guidedog projects and language editions into one website from a root ``conf.toml``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:234
msgid "Every mount ``source`` must resolve beneath the site configuration's directory after following symbolic links. An edition uses gettext catalogs in the mount's source project unless ``source`` names a separate translated project directory. Edition ``output`` is relative to the mount's ``output`` prefix; an empty string mounts at the root of that prefix. Mount names and edition output directories must be distinct ignoring ASCII case, though different mounts may share an output directory when their emitted files are disjoint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:238
msgid "Root conf.toml declares mounts, language editions, and shared assets; ssg build compiles each mount, verifies mount:// links and file collisions, and commits the assembled site in one Generation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:238
msgid "Mounted builds stage every project and language edition under one clock and publish the complete website atomically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:260
msgid "Supported mount builders are ``html``, ``dirhtml``, ``pdf`` and ``discussion-pdf`` (which validates a discussion catalog under ``prefix`` and builds a PDF book for each active record). ``exclude`` filters a mount's relative output files with globs, and ``overrides`` passes ``-D`` setting assignments (with ``{language}``, ``{edition}``, ``{site_root}`` and ``{base_url}`` placeholders). Select languages through ``editions.language`` rather than a ``language`` override so routing and translated output stay aligned. Nonempty ``version`` and ``release`` values from the root configuration propagate to every mount, and one publication clock governs the entire build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:262
msgid "Link across mounts with the ``mount://`` scheme:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:268
msgid "The link resolves to the target mount's matching language edition, falling back to its default edition. HTML receives a relative URL (preserving any hosting prefix), while PDFs use the public ``base_url``. Assembly verifies that every ``mount://`` target exists in the current generation, so a stale file from an earlier build cannot satisfy a link to a newly withheld document. If any mount fails or a cross-mount target is missing, the previous published website remains intact."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:270
msgid "Pass ``--no-pdf`` to skip PDF mounts during an HTML preview and strip cross-mount PDF download links; custom templates can inspect ``ssg_no_pdf`` to hide PDF controls. Guidedog's own ``docs/conf.toml`` declares the landing pages, seven manual HTML editions, seven manual PDF books and discussion catalog builds in this format, and ``cargo xtask build-site`` delegates directly to ``guidedog ssg build``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:272
msgid "Pinned themes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:275
msgid "Package a directory containing ``_templates/`` and ``_static/`` into a portable ``.gdt`` archive and install it with an explicit SHA-256 pin:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:282
msgid "Replace ``HASH`` with the archive's SHA-256 digest. Enable the verified theme by setting ``theme = true`` in ``[site]``. Project ``_templates/`` files take precedence over the installed theme, and project static files override theme static files of the same relative path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:284
msgid "A version-one ``.gdt`` archive is UTF-8 JSON (``format = 1``) mapping portable relative paths beneath ``_templates/`` and ``_static/`` to base64 file bytes. Archives are bounded to 4,096 files, 64 MiB of expanded data, and 32 directory levels; traversal, case aliases, reserved platform names, symlinks, file/directory overlaps and executable hooks are rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/static-publishing.rst:286
msgid "``guidedog-theme.lock`` records the source URL or path, the archive SHA-256 and the canonical tree SHA-256 (computed over a versioned domain tag and length-delimited sorted filenames and file bytes). Installation writes and syncs ``_themes/<TREE_SHA256>/`` and verifies both the directory and the encoded lock before atomically replacing ``guidedog-theme.lock``. Builds never fetch themes over the network; they verify the locked tree offline and fail closed if any file is missing, modified, added or replaced by a symlink."
msgstr ""
